{"hits": {"hits": [{"created": "2026-10-20T01:33:00.940781+00:00", "modified": "2026-10-20T01:33:01.192056+00:00", "id": 23487357, "conceptrecid": "23272655", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23487357", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.23272655", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23487357", "metadata": {"title": "The Empty Occupant: Answerability, Testimony, and the Limits of Machine-Mediated Authority", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23487357", "publication_date": "2026-10-20", "description": "<p>An institution can issue a determination in seconds and take months to reconsider it.</p>\n<p>Now put a conversational AI in front of that institution. In the book's fictional test case, an insurer's AI appeals agent tells a policyholder, in the first person: <em>\"I have reconsidered my decision. I have reversed it and will honor this correction in future cases.\"</em> Nothing happens. The insurer's claims and recovery systems reject any stay or reversal the agent proposes unless the corporate appeals office separately signs it, because the agent never held the power to grant one. Who, exactly, answered?</p>\n<p><em>The Empty Occupant: Answerability, Testimony, and the Limits of Machine-Mediated Authority</em> is a book-length answer. It argues that automated decisions can scale faster than institutions' capacity to justify, reconsider, correct, or repair them, and it gives conceptual tools for saying precisely where that failure lies. Is the interface a bearer of commitments at all? Does the institution behind it own the position? Was adopting it justified? Can anyone actually sustain the answering at that scale? These are separable questions, and the book's central claim is that evidence for one does not by itself settle another.</p>\n<h3>The central thesis</h3>\n<p>Presumptive institutional authority requires effective Answerability at the grain and scale of its exercise. Accuracy, fluency, attribution, and nominal human oversight are not substitutes. What matters is whether there are routes through which reasons can actually change institutional commitments and their consequences.</p>\n<h3>What the book builds</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Discursive Ontology and Answerability.</strong> Defines commitment-bearing as the conjunction of four public relations (attribution continuity, challenge-responsive reconsideration, repair competence, and standing-consequence), argued to be necessary for the role and, defeasibly, sufficient. It distinguishes Actual, Residual, Prospective, and Empty Occupancy: a role's present, historical, developing, and missing bearership. It defends the axiom that missing bearership is not defective ownership: an institution cannot escape ownership of its commitments by pointing to an interface that never bore them, and its own failure to answer is not evidence that it never owned them. It also gives a three-part test (Evidential Independence, Adversarial Defeatability, Normative Effectiveness) for when an automated review counts as genuine reconsideration, and it traces these questions through model succession and agentic chains.</li>\n<li><strong>Testimony Without a Testifier.</strong> Separates what a hearer can learn from a machine output from who owns the claim. On assurance-based accounts, outputs can exercise testimonial force without constituting testimony. Reliability, fluency, and conversational memory do not by themselves make an interface the owner of a commitment.</li>\n<li><strong>Normative Sponsorship.</strong> Five conditions for warranted institutional adoption (epistemic access, authority, review bandwidth, independent rejection, repair power) and an opacity threshold set at contestation grain, so institutions need not achieve complete transparency. It separates technical opacity from organizational capture, where reasons are knowable but cannot bind.</li>\n<li><strong>Deontic Debt and Institutional Insolvency.</strong> Derives a service requirement from the defeasibility of presumptive authority. Deontic Debt is matured obligations deficiently serviced, not just a backlog. Institutional Insolvency is a scale-indexed finding that no credible service plan exists under a specified lawful architecture and horizon. Its consequence is the targeted loss of presumptive authority, not automatic legal invalidity and not release from ownership and repair duties.</li>\n<li><strong>Ongoing stewardship.</strong> A four-level scheme of use, from tool use to unsponsorable operative force, tied to protective review, binding reconsideration, effective repair, and staged restoration after failure. It also maps intersections with administrative-law principles in Australia, with comparative EU, UK, and US material.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Tested against the record</h3>\n<p>Public-record analyses of Robodebt, <em>Moffatt v. Air Canada</em>, <em>A.M.C. v. Smith</em> (TennCare), the Post Office Horizon scandal, the Dutch childcare-benefits affair, and a press-based DPD chatbot episode show what the framework can and cannot establish from documents. The Air Canada and DPD records leave the decisive questions indeterminate, and the book says so.</p>\n<p>An explicitly fictional comparator, the Meridian&ndash;Aster appeals case, supplies the determinate verdict: the AI agent Aster is an Empty Occupant, and its insurer, Meridian, remains the actual bearer who owns the refusal. The pair of findings is the book's distinctive payoff. It is not empirical evidence, and it settles neither sponsorship nor serviceability.</p>\n<h3>Built to be challenged</h3>\n<p>A full chapter of objections takes on synthetic agency, status-function constructivism, intentionality, the human&ndash;machine double standard, scarcity, strategic contestation, and the charge that insolvency is trivially curable. It closes with explicit conditions under which the book would be wrong. Appendices provide a conceptual glossary, a proposed public-marker and obligation audit specification, and a worked obligation ledger. The ledger makes missing evidence explicit rather than filling it in with theory.</p>\n<p><strong>Scope:</strong> this is philosophical reconstruction with bounded public-record analysis. The documentary cases do not validate a diagnostic instrument, the equations are uncalibrated, and nothing here entails universal thresholds or automatic legal consequences.</p>\n<h3>Who it is for</h3>\n<p>Philosophers of language, testimony, assertion, and responsibility; social epistemologists and social ontologists. AI-governance researchers and administrative lawyers. Public-sector auditors, regulators, and anyone who has been told by a machine that their problem was \"resolved.\"</p>\n<p>Open access under CC BY 4.0.</p>\n<h3>Earlier preprints and development of the framework</h3>\n<p>This consolidated, author-finalized monograph supersedes the earlier Zenodo and PhilArchive preprints:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cott, N. (2026a). Answerability and Institutional Commitment: Why Missing Bearership Is Not Defective Ownership. Zenodo. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23151128\">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23151128</a></li>\n<li>Cott, N. (2026b). Deontic Debt and Institutional Insolvency: Answerability and the Limits of Scalable Authority. Zenodo. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23175522\">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23175522</a></li>\n<li>Cott, N. (2026c). Empty Occupancy: Answerability and the Ownership of Machine-Mediated Institutional Commitments. Zenodo. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23149754\">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23149754</a></li>\n<li>Cott, N. (2026d). Normative Sponsorship Under Opacity: When Institutions May Own Machine-Mediated Commitments. Zenodo. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23151252\">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23151252</a></li>\n<li>Cott, N. (2026e). Testimony Without a Testifier: Epistemic Value and Commitment Ownership in Institutional AI. Zenodo. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23153024\">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23153024</a></li>\n<li>Cott, N. (2026f). When Does Automated Review Constitute Genuine Reconsideration? Zenodo. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23307513\">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23307513</a></li>\n</ul>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Cott, Nicholas", "affiliation": "Independent", "orcid": "0009-0006-5433-1296"}], "keywords": ["Artificial Intelligence", "AI Governance", "Algorithmic Accountability", "Automated Decision-Making", "Institutional Authority", "Philosophy of Artificial Intelligence", "Social Epistemology", "Discursive Ontology", "Philosophy of Language", "Answerability", "Discursive Occupancy", "Empty Occupancy", "Commitment Ownership", "Institutional Testimony", "Normative Sponsorship", "Algorithmic Opacity", "Institutional Insolvency", "Deontic Debt", "Organizational Capture", "Discursive Stewardship"], "related_identifiers": [{"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.23175522", "relation": "isNewVersionOf", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}, {"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.23151252", "relation": "isNewVersionOf", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}, {"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.23153024", "relation": "isNewVersionOf", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}, {"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.23151128", "relation": "isNewVersionOf", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}, {"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.23149754", "relation": "isNewVersionOf", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}], "references": ["Alfrink, K., Keller, I., Doorn, N., & Kortuem, G. (2023). Contestable AI by design: Towards a framework. Minds and Machines, 33, 613\u2013639. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11023-022-09611-z", "Arora, C. (2024). Proxy assertions and agency: The case of machine-assertions. Philosophy & Technology, 37, Article 15. https://doi.org/10.1007/s13347-024-00703-5", "Bansal, G., Wu, T., Zhou, J., Fok, R., Nushi, B., Kamar, E., Ribeiro, M. T., & Weld, D. S. (20